WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.750
A practical cryptography baseline for .

2
00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:06.700
NET developers starts with the security property needed.

3
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:20.734
Decide whether the application needs confidentiality, integrity, authenticity, password verification, transport protection, proof of origin, or protected workflow state before choosing the mechanism.

4
00:00:20.734 --> 00:00:22.144
Use maintained .

5
00:00:22.144 --> 00:00:23.555
NET and ASP.

6
00:00:23.555 --> 00:00:27.575
NET Core APIs rather than custom algorithms or protocols.

7
00:00:27.575 --> 00:00:44.924
Prefer framework password hashing for passwords, authenticated encryption or Data Protection where appropriate, certificate validation for service identity, and cryptographically secure randomness for keys, salts, tokens, nonces, and identifiers.

8
00:00:44.924 --> 00:00:47.251
Protect, rotate, and govern keys.

9
00:00:47.251 --> 00:00:54.726
Keep secrets out of source, logs, public configuration, client assets, screenshots, and support artifacts.

10
00:00:54.726 --> 00:01:03.119
Separate environments, limit access, plan rotation, and prepare containment and communication steps for key compromise.

11
00:01:03.119 --> 00:01:04.529
Configure ASP.

12
00:01:04.529 --> 00:01:07.914
NET Core Data Protection for deployment reality.

13
00:01:07.914 --> 00:01:20.961
Multi-instance applications, containers, cloud hosting, deployment slots, and durable key storage can all affect cookies, antiforgery tokens, reset tokens, and other protected payloads.

14
00:01:20.961 --> 00:01:25.051
Finally, review production behavior and incident response.

15
00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:28.860
Cryptography is not only about selecting an algorithm.

16
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:40.214
It is about using the right maintained mechanism, protecting the keys, validating trust, avoiding unsafe logging, and operating the system responsibly over time.
